@node Install from MELPA
@section Install from MELPA

@cindex MELPA

After the package is on MELPA, add MELPA to @code{package-archives} and
then use @code{use-package}.

@lisp
(use-package latex-speed-commands
  :ensure t
  :hook ((LaTeX-mode . latex-speed-commands-mode)
         (latex-mode . latex-speed-commands-mode)))
@end lisp

@node Install from source
@section Install from source

@cindex installation from source

Clone the repository and put it on the load path.

@example
git clone https://github.com/MooersLab/latex-speed-commands-el.git
@end example

@lisp
(add-to-list 'load-path "/path/to/latex-speed-commands-el")
(require 'latex-speed-commands)
(add-hook 'LaTeX-mode-hook #'latex-speed-commands-mode) ; AUCTeX
(add-hook 'latex-mode-hook #'latex-speed-commands-mode) ; built-in
@end lisp

To read this manual inside Emacs from a source checkout, run @code{make
info} and then type @code{C-u C-h i} followed by the path of
@samp{docs/latex-speed-commands.info}.

@node Requirements
@section Requirements

@cindex requirements

The package needs Emacs 28.1 or newer, because it uses
@code{outline-cycle} and @code{outline-cycle-buffer}.  AUC@TeX{} is optional.

@node Getting started
@chapter Getting started

@cindex getting started

Open a @LaTeX{} file and turn on the minor mode with @code{M-x
latex-speed-commands-mode}, unless a hook already did.  The mode
line shows @samp{Speed}.  Then try these steps.

@enumerate
@item
Move point to the very beginning of a @code{\section@{...@}} line.
@item
Press @code{?}.  A help buffer lists every speed command.
@item
Press @code{n} and @code{p} to move between headings.
@item
Press @code{c} to fold and unfold the section.
@item
Press @code{F} to jump to the next environment.  On that @code{\begin} line,
press @code{e} to jump to the matching @code{\end} and @code{e} again to jump back.
@item
Move point one character to the right and press @code{n}.  The letter
is inserted, because point is no longer at a speed position.
@end enumerate

To insert one of the speed keys literally at a speed position, type
@code{C-q} first, for example @code{C-q n}.  This is the same trick that works
in Org.

@node Speed positions
@chapter Speed positions

@cindex speed position
@cindex heading line
@cindex environment line

A @emph{speed position} is a place where the speed keys are active.  There
are two kinds by default, and a third kind is optional.

@menu
* Heading lines::
* Environment lines::
* Commented headings and environments::
* Section boundaries::
* Speed keys at every line start::
@end menu

@node Heading lines
@section Heading lines

@cindex heading line, definition
@cindex sectioning commands
@vindex latex-speed-commands-section-levels

Point must be at the beginning of a line that starts with one of the
commands in @code{latex-speed-commands-section-levels}.  The line may be
indented.  The command may be starred, and it may have an optional
argument, as in @code{\section*[Short]@{Long title@}}.  The keys in
@code{latex-speed-commands} are active there.

The default levels are listed in the next table.

@float Table,orgf35be99
@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaa}
@headitem Command
@tab Level
@item @code{\part}
@tab 0
@item @code{\chapter}
@tab 1
@item @code{\section}
@tab 2
@item @code{\subsection}
@tab 3
@item @code{\subsubsection}
@tab 4
@item @code{\paragraph}
@tab 5
@item @code{\subparagraph}
@tab 6
@end multitable
@caption{Default sectioning commands and their outline levels.}
@end float

Add your own entries for other classes.  For Beamer slides you might
add @code{("frametitle" . 7)}.

@node Environment lines
@section Environment lines

@cindex environment line, definition

Point must be at the beginning of a line that starts with
@code{\begin@{NAME@}} or @code{\end@{NAME@}}.  The keys in
@code{latex-speed-commands-environment} are active there.  These play the
role of the Babel speed keys on an Org @code{#+begin_src} line.

When a line is both an environment line and covered by the optional
predicate described below, the environment keys win.

@node Commented headings and environments
@section Commented headings and environments

@cindex commented headings
@cindex COMMENT, Org analog

A heading or environment line that starts with a percent sign, as in
@code{% \section@{Old draft@}}, is still a speed position.  This is the
@LaTeX{} analog of an Org @code{COMMENT} headline, and it lets the @code{#} key
uncomment what it commented.  Navigation counts commented headings
too.

@node Section boundaries
@section Section boundaries

@cindex section boundaries
@vindex latex-speed-commands-section-boundary-regexp

A section extends to the next heading of the same or a higher rank.
It also stops at @code{\end@{document@}}, @code{\appendix}, @code{\frontmatter},
@code{\mainmatter}, @code{\backmatter}, @code{\bibliography@{...@}} and
@code{\printbibliography}.  The option
@code{latex-speed-commands-section-boundary-regexp} holds this list.
Without it, killing the last chapter would also kill
@code{\end@{document@}}.

@node Speed keys at every line start
@section Speed keys at every line start

@cindex leftmost column
@cindex beginning of line
@vindex latex-speed-commands-use

Like @code{org-use-speed-commands}, the option @code{latex-speed-commands-use}
may hold a function.  When the function returns non-nil, the heading
speed keys are active at point, in addition to the usual positions.

@lisp
;; Speed keys at the start of every line.
(setq latex-speed-commands-use #'bolp)
@end lisp

With this setting, a paragraph can no longer begin with a speed key
letter unless you type @code{C-q} first.  Many people prefer a narrower
predicate, such as one that also requires the line to be empty.

@lisp
(setq latex-speed-commands-use
      (lambda () (and (bolp) (eolp))))
@end lisp

All heading commands act on the section around point, so they also
work from inside a section body.

